#import "/modern-iaf/manuals/layout/manual.typ": *

#idx("Phase 3")#idx("Navigation")Phase 3 of the training course, "Navigation and instruments", takes you from a route of steerpoints at altitude to the low-level route, the instrument approach in cloud and the systems failures. It has six #term("lesson")s: four day lessons, a night lesson and check ride 3, flown at night. Each starts cold and dark on the parking spot and ends with the engine shut down on that spot. The briefing and the #term("notebook")'s Lesson tab give each lesson's route and the figures to hold; the navigation systems and the failures are in each jet's flight manual: the #other("f16i", "navigation") and #other("f16i", "emergency-systems"), the #other("f16d", "navigation") and #other("f16d", "emergency-systems"), the #other("f35i", "navigation") and #other("f35i", "emergency-systems").

=== The lessons <navigation-lessons>

#idx("Phase 3, lessons")
#grid-table(
  (auto, 2fr, 1fr),
  ([Lesson], [What it teaches], [Graded]),
  [3.1 Waypoint navigation], [A route of four steerpoints at 15,000 ft, on its planned times, then a straight-in landing.], [the distance from each steerpoint, the height held, the time at the last steerpoint],
  [3.2 Low-level navigation], [The same at 500 ft above the ground: the eyes outside, the steering a glance.], [the distance from each steerpoint, the height above the ground, the time at the last steerpoint],
  [3.3 Instrument approach], [In cloud: the departure on the instruments, the TACAN to two fixes on the runway's line, the ILS to the decision height and the landing.], [the localizer's and the glide slope's deviation, the approach speed, the touchdown point],
  [3.4 Systems failures], [Failures one after the other on the way out: in the F-16s the main generator, the flight controls' standby gains, both generators with the EPU started by hand, and hydraulic system B; in the F-35I a stuck landing gear, the electrical failure and the degraded flight controls. Then home, straight in.], [the failures' steps done, the height held while working],
  [3.5 At night], [Lesson 3.2's route by night at 1,000 ft above the ground, with the goggles or the helmet's night camera.], [the distance from each steerpoint, the height above the ground],
  [3.6 Check ride 3], [Everything of phases 1 to 3 at night, with no calls in flight.], [phases 1 to 3's items],
)

Each item is graded against its standard. The failures' steps are on the notebook's EMERGENCY tab.

=== Check ride 3 <navigation-check>

#idx("Check ride")The instructor gives the briefing and the #term("debrief") only: no calls and no switch highlights in flight; the notebook stays with you. The ride flies at night: two steerpoints at altitude, two at low level, the two fixes and the ILS. One emergency already taught (from phases 2 and 3) may come at a random time, unannounced. The ride grades the items of phases 1 to 3, the night lighting included, and an emergency's items only when that emergency came.
